  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Interpreting $x(t-T)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret $x(t-T)$ for $T > 0 by mapping each original feature time t0 to t0 + T and recognizing the result as a T-unit delay$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting $x(t-T)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Interpreting $x(t-T)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When $T>0$, reading $x(t-T)$ means the signal is delayed by $T$ time units. The minus sign inside the argument tells you to move the waveform to the right, not to the left.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To see the rule on one feature, suppose a particular feature of $x(t)$ occurs at time $t_0$. In the shifted signal, that same feature appears later, at $t_0+T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t_0\\mapsto t_0+T",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Nothing about the shape or values changes. The only change is time: each original feature time moves right by $T$, so $x(t-T)$ is a $T$-unit delay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting $x(t-T)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing $x(t-T)$ as a Delay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $y(t)=x(t-4)$, decide whether the signal is delayed or advanced and state the amount of shift.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $x(t-T)$ with $T>0$, the cue is the minus sign inside the argument. That sign means delay: the signal keeps the same shape, but every feature moves right by $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t-4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In this example, the shift amount is $T=4$. So the rule says each original feature time $t_0$ moves to $t_0+4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t_0\\mapsto t_0+4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $y(t)=x(t-4)$ is a $4$-unit delay. The waveform is unchanged except for the rightward shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting $x(t-T)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Mapping One Feature Time to $t_0+T$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A signal $x(t)$ has a spike at $t_0=2$. Where does that spike appear in $y(t)=x(t-5)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The spike at $t_0=2$ is the feature to track, and the cue is still the delay rule for $x(t-T)$ with $T>0$. Subtracting a positive amount inside the argument shifts that feature right by $T$ without changing its value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here $T=5$, so the feature time moves from $t_0=2$ to $t_0+T=2+5=7$. The waveform value at the spike stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t_0+T=2+5=7",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the spike appears at $t=7$, and $y(t)$ is a $5$-unit delay of $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting $x(t-T)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Mapping Multiple Feature Times in $x(t-T)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Original feature times are at $t=1$, $t=3$, and $t=7$. Locate them in $y(t)=x(t-2)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same delay rule: with $x(t-T)$ and $T>0$, add $T$ to every original feature time. Because the same shift is applied to each feature, the spacing between features stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t_i\\mapsto t_i+T",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the original feature times are $1$, $3$, and $7$, and the shift is $T=2$. So each time becomes $1+2$, $3+2$, and $7+2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\{1,3,7\\}\\mapsto\\{3,5,9\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the features appear at $3$, $5$, and $9$, and the order and spacing are unchanged. $y(t)=x(t-2)$ is a $2$-unit delay of $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Interpreting $x(t-T)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Mapping Support Intervals in $x(t-T)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)$ is nonzero on $0 \\le t < 2$, what is the support interval of $y(t)=x(t-3)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the support interval of $x(t)$, and the rule is the same delay rule used for feature times: when $x(t-T)$ has $T>0$, add $T$ to each endpoint and keep each endpoint's open or closed status.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "[0,2)\\mapsto[3,5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the original support interval is $0 \\le t < 2$ and the shift amount is $T=3$. So the left endpoint becomes $0+3=3$ and the right endpoint becomes $2+3=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "0\\le t<2\\mapsto 3\\le t<5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the support interval of $y(t)=x(t-3)$ is $3 \\le t < 5$. The closed-open structure stays the same, and the interval is shifted right by $3$ units.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
